nodes.statement:
Let $R$ be an ind-smooth $\mathbb{F}_p$-algebra. Then, for each $n \geq 0$, one has:  egin{enumerate} \item (Geisser--Levine \cite{GL}) There is a natural map $\pi_n ( K(R)/p) ightarrow 
u^n(R)$, which is an isomorphism if $R$ is local. \item (Geisser--Hesselholt \cite{GH}) There is a functorial exact sequence $0 	o \widetilde{
u}^{n+1}(R) 	o \pi_n ( \mathrm{TC}(R)/p) 	o {
u}^{n}(R) 	o 0$. Furthermore, under these identifications, the composite of $\pi_n ( K(R)/p) 	o \pi_n (\mathrm{TC}(R)/p) 	o 
u^n(R) $ (where the first map arises from the cyclotomic trace) is the map of (1). \item If $R$ is local, we have a functorial identification $\pi_n ( K^{\mathrm{inv}}(R)/p) \simeq \widetilde{
u}^{n+2}(R)$. \end{enumerate}

nodes.statement_short:
For ind-smooth $R$: $K/p$ is computed by $
u^n$, $\mathrm{TC}/p$ by an extension of $
u^n$ by $\widetilde{
